WEBVTT
TITLE Mapping Reductions and Rice's Theorem: Which Way the Arrow Points, and Where the Theorem Stops — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A reduction is a translation, not a comparison. You rewrite one question as another question.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Two questions to keep apart. The acceptance problem: does M accept the string w? The halting problem: does M stop on w at all?

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Nobody compares the two problems directly. One instance of the first is turned into one instance of the second.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
A machine and a string become a different machine and a string. The string may be copied; the machine is rebuilt.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The arrow points from the problem you give up on to the problem you assume solved. Read it as: no harder than.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
First obligation: the function must be computable, which means one Turing machine that halts on every input and leaves the answer on the tape.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Then A is mapping reducible to B when there is such a function with: w is in A exactly when f of w is in B.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Exactly when is two directions. Yes stays yes and no stays no. A one-way implication is not a reduction.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Both directions together are one sentence: A is the preimage of B under f. Nothing about the sizes of the two sets is claimed.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Why totality matters: to decide A you run f and then the decider for B. If f might not stop, the procedure is not a decider.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Law one, good news travels backwards. If A reduces to B and B is decidable, then A is decidable.

12
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Law two, bad news travels forwards. If A reduces to B and A is undecidable, then B is undecidable.

13
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Same hypothesis, opposite conclusions. Decidability is inherited by the left end. Undecidability is inherited by the right end.

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
So the trap: from A reduces to B, and B undecidable, nothing follows about A. The arrow tells you about the left end, not the right.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
The rest is the same picture: recognisability travels backwards, non-recognisability forwards, and the complement of A reduces to the complement of B by the same f.

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Here is a claim worth testing with a computation: if L reduces to the acceptance problem, then L is undecidable.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Take L to be the empty language. Send every string to one fixed instance, the machine that rejects at once paired with the string zero.

18
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Computed on all fifteen strings of length at most three: no string is in the empty language, and no image is in the acceptance problem. The biconditional holds fifteen times out of fifteen.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
So the empty language reduces to the acceptance problem. And the empty language is decidable: one machine, reject everything, one step.

20
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The right end of the arrow can be as hard as you like. Difficulty travels only to the right, so the right end never constrains the left.

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Now the honest direction. To show the acceptance problem reduces to the halting problem, given M and w, build M-prime.

22
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
M-prime ignores its own input. It runs M on w. If M accepts, M-prime accepts. If M rejects, M-prime loops forever.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Then M-prime halts on w exactly when M accepts w. The if-and-only-if is built into the loop, not checked afterwards.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
The infinite loop is not a defect. It is the construction: it turns a rejection into a non-halting, which is the only difference the two problems have.

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
And the description of M-prime is produced by a fixed procedure from M and w, so the function f is computable and total.

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Seven instances, five machines, and the simulation really runs. Each row is one instance of the acceptance problem.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
The first machine accepts one, so its wrapper halts. It rejects zero, so its wrapper never halts.

28
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
The reject-everything machine rejects zero at once, so its wrapper loops. The looping machine never stops, so its wrapper never stops either.

29
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
The correspondence holds on seven rows out of seven. Note the three rows that halt and do not accept: in the halting problem, not in the acceptance problem.

30
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
A four hundred step cap can only say: no acceptance within four hundred steps. The seven instances are chosen so that this equals the truth.

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Reverse it. To reduce halting to acceptance, build M-double-prime, which runs M on w and accepts whatever happens, as long as it halts.

32
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Then M-double-prime accepts w exactly when M halts on w. Computed on the same seven instances: seven out of seven again.

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Both arrows hold, so the two problems are equivalent under the order. Equally hard, in the only sense the order can express.

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
The order is reflexive and transitive. The identity function proves reflexivity; composition of computable functions proves transitivity. Both checked on fifteen strings.

35
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Transitivity is what lets a proof chain. Any language reducing to acceptance also reduces to halting, in one composed step.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Two machines. Run both on all fifteen strings of length at most three. Each accepts exactly seven, and the seven are the same seven.

37
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
One machine has four states, the other seven. Three unreachable states were bolted on and the language did not move.

38
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
So the property has at most five states is true of one and false of the other, while their languages are the same on every string tested.

39
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
Rice: every non-trivial property of the language of a machine is undecidable. A property of the language cannot be read off the description.

40
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Non-trivial means some language has it and some language does not. That is the theorem's only hypothesis, and the only thing to check.

41
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Rice says nothing about syntactic properties. Has four states, has at least twenty-one transitions, reaches q in exactly one hundred steps: all decidable.

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Nor about trivial ones. The language is recognisable is true of every machine; the language is not recognisable is true of none. Both decidable.

43
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Rice gives undecidability, never unrecognisability. The language is non-empty is undecidable and still recognisable: run the machine on every string in parallel.

44
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
The language is empty is not even recognisable. Undecidable and unrecognisable are two verdicts, and a question can ask for either.

45
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Two blank-tape questions, different answers: halts on the empty string is undecidable; has at least two thousand and sixteen states is decidable.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Four exam shapes: decide a property of a language, construct a reduction, spot a wrong direction, and sort several languages into decidable and not.

47
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Shape one, a property of the language: Rice, unless the property is trivial or syntactic. Check those two exclusions before anything else.

48
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Shape two, comparing two machines: equality of their languages is a semantic property of the pair, non-trivial, so undecidable.

49
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Shape three, the exception that looks semantic: takes more than one thousand and seventy-three steps on every string is decidable, because in that many steps the head sees only that many symbols.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
The line to carry: the direction of the arrow is the argument. Everything else on the page is decoration around that one arrow.

